Preserve the bytecode pointer while an unwind-jump destination is on the object stack.

MP_BC_UNWIND_JUMP temporarily stores its destination ip as an mp_obj_t. The original
conversion routes the pointer through uintptr_t, which retains the numeric address but drops
out-of-band capability metadata. When unwind_jump later pops that value through MP_OBJ_TO_PTR,
the interpreter installs an untagged ip and faults on the next bytecode fetch.

The port uses object representation A, where mp_obj_t is a pointer, and the matching pop
already uses MP_OBJ_TO_PTR. Use MP_OBJ_FROM_PTR for the push so the capability remains intact.
This is the control-flow path exercised by break/continue through with/finally blocks.

diff --git a/py/vm.c b/py/vm.c
--- a/py/vm.c
+++ b/py/vm.c
@@ -685,7 +685,10 @@ unwind_jump:;
                 ENTRY(MP_BC_UNWIND_JUMP): {
                     MARK_EXC_IP_SELECTIVE();
                     DECODE_SLABEL;
-                    PUSH((mp_obj_t)(mp_uint_t)(uintptr_t)(ip + slab)); // push destination ip for jump
+                    /* The destination is still a pointer. Routing it through uintptr_t keeps
+                       only its address on capability targets, so the later MP_OBJ_TO_PTR(POP())
+                       reconstructs an untagged ip and faults at the next dispatch. */
+                    PUSH(MP_OBJ_FROM_PTR(ip + slab)); // push destination ip for jump
                     PUSH((mp_obj_t)(mp_uint_t)(*ip)); // push number of exception handlers to unwind (0x80 bit set if we also need to pop stack)
 unwind_jump:;
                     mp_uint_t unum = (mp_uint_t)POP(); // get number of exception handlers to unwind
